  <%# ── Main prose column ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── %>
  <div class="about-content">

    <h1 id="what-is-enliterator">What is Enliterator?</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      Enliterator confers <strong>literacy</strong> on a collection. Not search — literacy: the capacity to
      participate in inquiry, to accumulate understanding, to compound across visits the way a reader
      compounds across books. A searchable archive can find words. An <em>enliterated</em> collection can
      think about itself.
    </p>

    <%# The page demonstrates its own thesis: it speaks about the collection it's mounted on. %>
    <% if @stats && @stats[:visits].to_i.positive? %>
      <div class="stats-strip">
        <div class="stats-eyebrow">This collection, right now</div>
        <div class="stats-grid">
          <div class="stat-cell">
            <span class="stat-num"><%= number_with_delimiter @stats[:tended_records] %></span>
            <span class="stat-label">records tended</span>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-cell">
            <span class="stat-num"><%= number_with_delimiter @stats[:visits] %></span>
            <span class="stat-label">tending visits</span>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-cell">
            <span class="stat-num"><%= number_with_delimiter @stats[:live_claims] %></span>
            <span class="stat-label">things it knows</span>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-cell">
            <span class="stat-num"><%= @stats[:facets].size %></span>
            <span class="stat-label">facets of inquiry</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <% if @stats[:facets].any? %>
          <div class="stats-facets">
            <span class="facets-label">Active facets:</span>
            <% @stats[:facets].each do |s| %>
              <span class="facet-chip"><%= s %></span>
            <% end %>
          </div>
        <% end %>
        <% if (@stats[:vocab_resolved].to_i + @stats[:vocab_pending].to_i).positive? %>
          <div class="stats-footer">
            Vocabulary: <strong><%= number_with_delimiter @stats[:vocab_resolved] %></strong> requests resolved &middot;
            <%= number_with_delimiter @stats[:vocab_pending] %> open.
            The open count is evidence, not arrears: each request is <em>literary warrant</em> accumulating
            from reading, and most will fold into an existing term or be declined — only the genuinely
            new are held for a human. The vocabulary tends itself — see <%= link_to "Requests", suggestions_path %>.
          </div>
        <% end %>
      </div>
      <p class="muted samples" style="margin-top:.5rem; margin-bottom:1.75rem">
        These numbers are live. Everything below explains how they came to be, and what they become over time.
      </p>
    <% end %>

    <h2 id="one-record-before-and-after">One record, before and after</h2>
    <p>
      The claim is abstract until you watch it happen to a single record. Take one work off the shelf:
    </p>
    <div class="duo-row">
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-head"><strong>Before</strong> <span class="chip">the catalog record</span></div>
        <ul>
          <li>A title, an author line, a date, an abstract — the surrogate a catalog keeps.</li>
          <li>Findable by keyword; readable only by a person with the time to read it.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-head"><strong>After</strong> <span class="chip">the tended record</span></div>
        <ul>
          <li>What it argues, what it found, where it's limited, which other work it speaks to — held
            as claims in the collection's governed vocabulary, with the author under one authorized
            name rather than five spellings.</li>
          <li>Every claim carries provenance: which model read it, on which visit, attributed to what.</li>
          <li>Named gaps — what was looked for and not found, and why.</li>
          <li>A visit-by-visit history of what changed each time it was read, so deepening is
            visible rather than assumed.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>
      And the difference is <strong>measured, not asserted</strong>: a first-impression diagnostic asks
      a machine reader questions about the record under both conditions and scores the lift over the bare
      surrogate — in its first controlled run, roughly a sevenfold improvement in the reader's calibration,
      an effect that persisted even at the strongest model. Everything below is that difference at
      collection scale: the machinery that produces it, and the instruments for checking the machinery's work.
    </p>

    <h2 id="the-missing-word">The missing word</h2>
    <p>
      A child is born with the hardware for language, but it sits inert until someone opens the door —
      points at marks, ties them to sounds, ties sounds to the accumulated record of human thought. After
      that door opens, every book changes how they read the next one. Literacy isn't a skill; it's access
      to a loop that <strong>compounds across a lifetime</strong>. We decided that withholding it from a
      child capable of it is not a missing feature — it's neglect.
    </p>
    <p>
      There was no word for the same thing happening to <em>data</em>: a record gaining the capacity to
      participate in meaningful inquiry — not searched once, but <strong>tended</strong>, accumulating
      understanding across visits. So we named it. <strong>Enliteracy</strong>: to put into literacy. The
      prefix does the work — enable, enrich, enlighten. Each is a threshold crossed. This is the door, for
      a collection.
    </p>

    <h2 id="what-tended-means">What "tended" means</h2>
    <p>
      Most systems stop at <em>searchable</em>: embeddings, a vector index, questions in natural language.
      Useful — and the bare minimum. Searchable is to literate what recognizing letters is to reading. You
      can find the words; you don't yet understand the sentences.
    </p>
    <p>
      Enliterator runs a maintenance loop on each record — but the loop runs through a <strong>language
      model</strong>, not a heuristic. A cron job inspects and flags; it never learns. When the loop runs
      through language, each visit brings the whole compressed geometry of human thought to bear on
      <em>this</em> record in <em>this</em> state. The tenth visit isn't smarter because it remembers the
      previous nine — any database remembers. It's smarter because the corpus meets the accumulated history
      of this particular record, and the intersection produces understanding neither contained alone. That
      is what the child gets when the door opens: not a loop of looking at marks, but marks connected to a
      depth of thought no single lifetime could hold. The record gains the same connection.
    </p>

    <h2 id="compounding-attention">Compounding attention — now, and over time</h2>
    <p>
      This is the whole point, so it's worth being precise about what changes the day you turn it on, and
      what changes only with time.
    </p>
    <div class="duo-row">
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-head"><strong>Now</strong> <span class="chip">first pass</span></div>
        <ul>
          <li>A dormant archive becomes <strong>legible</strong>. A record that was only findable becomes
            readable — it carries what it argues, why it matters, who made it, what it connects to.</li>
          <li>The collection gains a <strong>self-portrait</strong>: it knows its own vocabulary, its gaps,
            its shape — and can answer questions grounded in its own records, with provenance.</li>
          <li><strong>First attention</strong> reaches records no curator ever had time for. The triage that
            left most of a collection untouched is no longer forced.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-head"><strong>Over time</strong> <span class="chip">the compounding</span></div>
        <ul>
          <li>Understanding <strong>deepens</strong>, not just persists. Each visit meets the record's
            accumulated history; claims are refined, not duplicated.</li>
          <li>The vocabulary <strong>converges</strong>. Synonyms fold together, noise is rejected, genuine
            new concepts become permanent — the collection's language sharpens toward what actually matters.</li>
          <li>The investment <strong>compounds</strong>. A stronger model or a richer vocabulary re-tends the
            whole collection and lifts all of it at once; cross-record connections thicken into a graph. The
            collection knows more next year than it does today.</li>
          <li>And this is <strong>measured, not assumed</strong>: every record carries its own
            "understanding over time" — visit-by-visit diffs of what it knows — and a blind judge
            compares earlier vs later understanding. If a re-visit merely restates, the system can
            tell; deepening has to be earned.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>
      And the economics flip. When the cost of examining one record drops low enough, the question is no
      longer <em>"can we afford to look at this?"</em> but <em>"can we afford <strong>not</strong> to?"</em>
      A collection of 160,000 works left intellectually dormant was once a resource constraint. When the
      constraint lifts, what remains is a choice.
    </p>

    <h2 id="how-it-works">How it works</h2>
    <p>The machinery, plainly — this is what the engine does today:</p>
    <div class="card">
      <table class="mech-table">
        <tr>
          <td class="mech-key"><strong>Facets are roles</strong></td>
          <td>Each facet of understanding is its own job<%= @stats && @stats[:facets].any? ? " — here: #{@stats[:facets].join(', ')}".html_safe : "" %>. A record is tended once per facet, so "what it argues" and "who wrote it" don't compete in one pass.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="mech-key"><strong>Tiers are capability</strong></td>
          <td>A cheap model does the first pass; the loop escalates to a stronger one only when confidence is low, with a verify floor guarding the result. You pay for capability exactly where it's needed.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="mech-key"><strong>Claims, reconciled</strong></td>
          <td>Each visit proposes claims and reconciles them against what's already known — add, update, delete, or leave alone — so re-tending <em>deepens</em> a record instead of duplicating it. Authoritative host facts are locked and never re-derived.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="mech-key"><strong>A vocabulary that tends itself</strong></td>
          <td>The vocabulary is governed the way a library governs a thesaurus (the Z39.19 discipline): the model can't invent terms — it proposes them by <em>literary warrant</em>. Each reader works with the vocabulary <em>in hand</em> — it sees the candidate terms other readers have already proposed and <strong>affirms</strong> one that fits rather than coining a synonym, so warrant accrues as the breadth of records that recognize a term. An editor — here the <em>considerer</em> — then ratifies a slate (fold synonyms, reject noise, hold genuine new concepts for a human). Approved terms go <strong>live</strong>, and the loop converges on the genuinely contested ones. Authority control as the library has always practiced it: built from reading, distributed across the readers, ratified centrally.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="mech-key"><strong>Collections within collections</strong></td>
          <td>A <em>context</em> is a lens: an item belongs to the whole collection and to any number of labeled sub-collections, each with its own facets and vocabulary, inheriting its ancestors' — the finding-aid principle. An Executive Order is read for its <em>supersession graph</em>; a CRS report for its <em>issue for Congress</em>; neither lens contaminates the other, and within a context a record reads against its fellow members, not the undifferentiated whole.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="mech-key"><strong>Provenance throughout</strong></td>
          <td>Every claim knows how it came to be known — which model, which visit, what it was attributed to. Nothing is asserted without a trail.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="mech-key"><strong>A smoke alarm</strong></td>
          <td>Tending health and spend are visible per facet, so a silent failure can't masquerade as success.</td>
        </tr>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 id="the-pulse">The pulse</h2>
    <p>
      For its first fourteen versions this engine was <strong>hand-cranked on purpose</strong> — every pass
      watched, because supervision is how you earn the right to automate. Then we ran the experiment: cohorts
      re-tended under controlled conditions, every diff measured, a blind judge scoring earlier against later.
      The verdict was precise. Re-visiting is safe — but re-reading a record whose surroundings haven't changed
      teaches nothing. What deepens understanding is <em>change</em>: new neighbors tended, a vocabulary that
      grew, a source that moved. And nothing rivals <strong>first attention</strong> — the untended shelf is
      where almost all the learning is.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the heartbeat that replaced the hand-crank is <strong>event-driven, not scheduled</strong>. Each cycle
      it works the untended frontier first, re-reads only the records whose world actually changed, and keeps
      the old "re-tend everything every N days" rule only as a safety net. Two instruments replace the watching
      eye: a <strong>spend cap enforced on actual tokens</strong> — a cycle cannot exceed its appropriation —
      and a <strong>ledger</strong>: every cycle is a record of what it planned, why each item was chosen, and
      what it cost; every visit points back to the cycle that caused it. The schedule itself has provenance.
      And the plan always shows its <strong>horizon</strong> — "35,000 records remaining ≈ 700 cycles at this
      budget" — so the pace of a collection's enliteracy is a decision someone owns, never an accident.
      The pulse is also <strong>pressable</strong>: the <%= link_to "Heartbeat", heartbeat_path %> surface
      triggers one cycle and shows it beating — each visit landing on the ledger as it happens.
      And when you need a <strong>specific</strong> pass rather than the frontier &mdash; refresh these three
      records, or whatever drifted in this one book &mdash; the <strong>directed pulse</strong> is the second
      door: <code>enliterator:pulse</code> tends a named or drifted set (and, where the host wires it, that
      book&rsquo;s syntheses) as one governed, ledgered cycle, marked <code>pulse</code> on the same ledger.
      Tending has two doors now &mdash; the pacemaker for what the collection surfaces, the pulse for what you
      ask of it &mdash; and both leave the same auditable trail.
    </p>
    <p>
      And before any record is read, the collection <strong>shelf-reads itself</strong> — the practice
      every library knows. Each cycle runs a conservation survey up digital preservation's ladder:
      is the item <em>present</em> (the link resolves), <em>intact</em> (the file is valid),
      <em>extractable</em> (usable text came out)? A dead link with a cached copy still circulates —
      the library kept a surrogate; a record with no readable text is pulled from the tending queues
      so nothing is spent reading what cannot be read. What can't be probed is <em>intelligibility</em> —
      and there the tending loop itself is the instrument: records that read fine but never yield
      understanding surface as their own pile. A <strong>conservator</strong> reviews every pile and writes,
      in plain language, what's wrong and what staff must do to bring those records into the loop —
      the condition report becomes a work queue for people, and when a record is repaired, the next
      survey simply notices. Resolution is measured, never asserted.
    </p>
    <p>
      And the catalog <strong>audits itself</strong>. Each cycle, an examiner re-reads a random sample of
      claims against their sources and renders a verdict — supported, unsupported, contradicted — with
      humans confirming or overruling on the <%= link_to "Review", review_path %> page. The accuracy of
      the whole claim store is <strong>published, per facet and per tier</strong>, alongside the examiner's own
      calibration against its human reviewers. Verdicts never age out when a claim is replaced, so
      re-reading can never launder the number; and when a person corrects a wrong claim, the correction
      is locked — the model can never quietly overwrite a librarian.
    </p>

    <h2 id="the-reference-desk">The reference desk</h2>
    <p>
      An enliterated collection can be <em>asked</em>. The <%= link_to "Chat", conversation_path %>
      surface is not a search box wearing a chat skin — it's a <strong>reference interview</strong>.
      The desk triages a question, hands it to a grounded specialist when one is better placed to
      answer, reasons its reply while you watch the work, and <strong>cites the records it
      consulted</strong> inline — each citation a link back to the record itself. When it finishes it
      offers the next good questions, reasoned from the answer rather than drawn from a fixed list, so
      the inquiry continues by pointing — the way a good librarian hears the question behind your
      question.
    </p>
    <p>
      Its <strong>voice is yours to shape</strong>. Each desk carries an institutional register the
      engine owns and a <em>persona</em> a curator can edit, version, and roll back — the same
      authority control that governs the collection's vocabulary, now turned on how the desk speaks.
      And it is safe to hand someone the voice because <strong>behavior does not live in the
      words</strong>: what the desk may read, that it must ground every claim, that it shows its
      provenance — these are enforced by the engine's loop, not by the prompt. You can rewrite every
      line of a persona and never loosen a single guarantee.
    </p>
    <p>
      And the conversations themselves are <strong>kept</strong>. Each exchange is retained as a
      first-class record and can be replayed from its own event stream — so a good answer can be shown
      again without being re-run, and the collection can, in time, <strong>tend its own
      conversations</strong> the way it tends its records: the engine turning its attention on the one
      surface where it speaks. The thesis, applied to itself.
    </p>

    <h2 id="the-conscience-is-human">The conscience is human</h2>
    <p>
      At Burning Man there's a saying: if you make eye contact with trash, it's yours — not because you
      made it, because you <em>saw</em> it. The obligation doesn't come from perception; it comes from the
      relationship the gaze creates. Once you see it, you can't unsee it, and the world fills with things
      asking for grace.
    </p>
    <p>
      The tending system has no ethics. It has a schedule and a credit card. It runs because someone decided
      the output justified the cost; cancel the card and it stops. The <strong>conscience belongs to the
      person who saw the collection sitting dormant and couldn't look away</strong> — the one who can see the
      need and authorize the spend. Society funds a child's literacy with buildings and teachers' salaries.
      The enliteracy of a collection is the same shape: someone decides the tending is worth paying for. The
      engine is infrastructure. The decision is yours.
    </p>

    <h2 id="for-a-collection-like-this-one">For a collection like this one</h2>
    <p>
      A homeland-security thesis archive is exactly the case that rewards tending. Each thesis is a dense
      argument that took an author a year to make — and then it sits, findable by keyword, rarely read.
      Tended, every thesis declares what it contributes, what it found, where it's limited, who wrote it,
      and which other work it speaks to. The collection becomes a <strong>living body of scholarship</strong>
      that can be questioned as a whole, that surfaces the connections no single reader would find, and that
      grows sharper every time the loop runs. Dormant becomes legible; an archive becomes a colleague.
    </p>

    <h2 id="the-four-surfaces">The surfaces</h2>
    <div class="surfaces-grid">
      <div class="card">
        <span class="surface-title"><%= link_to "Status", root_path %></span>
        <span class="surface-desc">The self-portrait — per-facet health, the vocabulary it uses, the connection graph, and drill-down into any single tended record.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="surface-title"><%= link_to "Catalog", catalog_path %></span>
        <span class="surface-desc">The holdings, browsable — search by meaning, browse by subject heading, wander the open stacks; every card shows what the collection knows.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="surface-title"><%= link_to "Chat", conversation_path %></span>
        <span class="surface-desc">The reference desk — a governed reference interview that triages, routes to grounded specialists, streams its reasoning, and cites the records it consulted.</span>
      </div>
      <% if Enliterator.configuration.chat_persona_editing %>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="surface-title"><%= link_to "Desks", desks_path %></span>
        <span class="surface-desc">Shape the desk's voice — edit each desk's persona, versioned with rollback and reset-to-seed; the engine register and org chart stay code-owned and read-only.</span>
      </div>
      <% end %>
      <% if Enliterator.configuration.chat_retention %>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="surface-title"><%= link_to "Conversations", conversations_path %></span>
        <span class="surface-desc">The kept conversations — browse, label, and replay retained exchanges from their own event stream; the corpus the desk will learn to tend.</span>
      </div>
      <% end %>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="surface-title"><%= link_to "Requests", suggestions_path %></span>
        <span class="surface-desc">Govern the vocabulary — review the terms the model wants, approve / map / reject, and watch the loop converge.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="surface-title"><%= link_to "Review", review_path %></span>
        <span class="surface-desc">The human anchor — confirm, overrule, or correct the examiner's verdicts on sampled claims; the audit's ground truth.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="surface-title"><%= link_to "Contexts", contexts_path %></span>
        <span class="surface-desc">Collections within collections — the context tree, each lens's facets and members; the nav switcher views every surface through any of them.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="surface-title"><%= link_to "Atlas", atlas_path %></span>
        <span class="surface-desc">The collection, drawn — records, the entities their claims name, every edge carrying its provenance; replay it to watch the collection learn.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="surface-title"><%= link_to "Heartbeat", heartbeat_path %></span>
        <span class="surface-desc">The pulse, pressable — trigger one tending cycle and watch it live: the queue, the spend against its budget, every visit as it lands on the ledger.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="surface-title"><%= link_to "Settings", settings_path %></span>
        <span class="surface-desc">The configuration — the org chart of facets and tiers, the effective vocabulary, and how the considerer decides.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="surface-title">About</span>
        <span class="surface-desc">This page. What we're building and why — kept current as the engine grows.</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="about-colophon" id="version-history">
      <p>
        Living document &middot; reflects Enliterator as of <strong>August 2026</strong>.
        The most recent changes:
      </p>
      <ul class="colophon-list">
        <li><strong>v0.75</strong> &mdash; the warrant gets a date. A search warrant expires unexecuted,
          because probable cause has a shelf life &mdash; and a checked claim is no different: the
          source under it can change, and until now &ldquo;confirmed against the current text&rdquo;
          and &ldquo;never re-examined since it was written&rdquo; looked identical in the record.
          Every reading now files its warrant paper &mdash; a fingerprint of exactly the text it read
          &mdash; so any claim can answer <em>has the ground moved under me since anything checked?</em>
          Three honest answers: still current, gone stale, or <em>unknown</em> &mdash; and unknown is
          never dressed up as either. The quality reviewer now spends its attention where the ground
          has moved, which is where review belongs. An instrument, deliberately not an alarm: staleness
          informs the examiner and the curator; nothing is auto-revoked, because the record of what was
          once checked is itself part of the history.</li>
        <li><strong>v0.74</strong> &mdash; terms that say what they are <em>not</em>. A cataloger&rsquo;s
          thesaurus has always carried scope notes &mdash; &ldquo;here, not there,&rdquo; &ldquo;use only
          when&hellip;&rdquo; &mdash; and now the vocabulary does too: a term can declare values that do
          not count (an order merely cited is not an order superseded) and the condition under which the
          term applies at all (on a document that changes nothing, the right entry is no entry). The
          census had shown a key that looked weak was really being asked on documents where the honest
          answer was silence. Both halves reach the reader <em>and</em> the quality reviewer from one
          source, so the collection&rsquo;s reader and its measuring instrument can never quietly
          disagree about what a term means. Verified on the hard case: shown the note, a re-reading
          dropped two long-standing misreads it had been faithfully preserving for months.</li>
        <li><strong>v0.73</strong> &mdash; the catalog learns to say &ldquo;nothing&rdquo; and make it
          stick. Removing a wrong claim used to be temporary: the next reading re-derived it, and the
          phantom returned &mdash; measured on a live collection, barely half of one remediation
          survived the week. Now a reviewer can rule that a field holds nothing: the ruling is a
          locked, human-signed entry the machine can never overwrite, the catalog stops escalating
          readers to chase a value that does not exist, and the recorded gap closes as
          <em>adjudicated</em> &mdash; answered, not missing; the answer is &ldquo;nothing,&rdquo;
          and that is knowledge. Two contradicting curators are a loud error, never a quiet win, and
          a later correction can still supply a value over the ruling &mdash; refusal stays smaller
          than revolution. Verified end to end: a census flagged the doubtful entries, a reviewer
          retracted two phantom advisors, and the very next reading left them retracted.</li>
        <li><strong>v0.72</strong> &mdash; the measuring instruments, audited and rebuilt. A three-day
          cross-collection campaign turned the engine&rsquo;s own quality instruments on themselves and
          found what any honest audit finds: one number computed three ways, a sampler with no power to
          rank what it sampled, a reviewer that flipped a coin whenever a reader had correctly said
          &ldquo;nothing,&rdquo; and work quietly queued for a worker that did not exist. Now there is
          one definition of the supported rate; a <em>census</em> that reads every claim on a facet
          instead of a sliver; a report that shows the collection-weighted and still-standing rates
          beside the never-ages-out process record, and says when a number rests on too few verdicts to
          rank anything; and a reviewer that knows what a claim of absence asserts &mdash; confirmable
          only against a complete source, refutable from any fragment. Correct restraint and damaging
          silence finally read as different things.</li>
        <li><strong>v0.71</strong> &mdash; the catalog stops asking the reader a question it cannot
          answer. When a fact that ought to be present is missing, the collection records <em>why</em>:
          the item never said it, or the copy we hold is damaged and lost it. Only a reader was being
          asked &mdash; and a reader is handed the copy, never the original, so a damaged copy looks
          to it exactly like an item that stayed silent. On this collection it guessed wrong five times
          in six, and each wrong guess sends someone to the wrong remedy: go ask an authority, when the
          answer was to re-scan the page. The condition survey already knew which copies were damaged.
          Now it says so, and its knowledge outranks the reader&rsquo;s impression &mdash; with the
          reader&rsquo;s original answer kept alongside, so the correction is visible rather than
          quietly substituted.</li>
        <li><strong>v0.70</strong> &mdash; auditioning a reader instead of taking its word. When the staff
          changes, the obvious question is whether the new reader is as good as the last one, and the
          obvious evidence &mdash; how sure the reader says it is &mdash; is no evidence at all: a
          reader who rates itself highly is confident, not correct. The collection can now hold an
          audition. Each candidate reads the same records, and every claim any of them makes is checked
          against the source by one reviewer who is never told which candidate wrote it. Nothing the
          candidates say is filed; the audition leaves no trace in the catalog. The result is scored on
          the same scale as the collection&rsquo;s standing quality review, and it reports how small the
          sample was, so a narrow win reads as a narrow win.</li>
        <li><strong>v0.68.1</strong> &mdash; and it now asks the right place. The first attempt read the
          reader&rsquo;s name off the reply itself, where the institution simply repeats back the role you
          asked for &mdash; so the record kept saying &ldquo;the careful one&rdquo; and never who that was.
          The answer lives in the staff directory instead, which the collection now consults and keeps
          for a few minutes at a time. What it can promise is honest: the directory as it reads today,
          not a receipt signed by the reader.</li>
        <li><strong>v0.68</strong> &mdash; the record now names the reader that actually answered. Readers
          are addressed by role (&ldquo;the careful one&rdquo;), and which model stands behind a role can be
          changed by the institution at any time &mdash; so a catalog that recorded only the role could not
          tell you, later, who had actually done the reading. Each visit and each quality review now keeps
          both: the role we asked for, and the reader that answered. A change of staff becomes part of the
          record instead of a silence in it.</li>
        <li><strong>v0.67</strong> &mdash; a reader too small for the record is no longer sent to read it:
          a collection can now declare how much each reader can hold, and a record too large for one
          starts with a reader that can take it whole &mdash; rather than being quietly trimmed to fit,
          which would have meant reporting an understanding built on a piece of the thing.</li>
        <li><strong>v0.66</strong> &mdash; polish under an outside reader&rsquo;s eye: this page shows the
          transformation first (one record, before and after) and interprets its own numbers, its history
          became the list you are scanning, the nav folds eleven surfaces into the field&rsquo;s families
          (Holdings &middot; Reference &middot; Governance &middot; Operations) with the current surface
          marked &mdash; and the Requests desk stops taking six seconds in a deep-warrant context, its
          counts now saying what they count (observations, accumulated across proposed terms).</li>
        <li><strong>v0.65</strong> &mdash; the directed pulse: tending gains a second door &mdash;
          <code>enliterator:pulse</code> tends a named or drifted set of records (and a book&rsquo;s
          syntheses, where the host wires it) as one governed cycle, marked on the ledger, for when you
          need a specific pass rather than the frontier.</li>
      </ul>
      <details class="colophon-more">
        <summary>The full history, v0.12 &ndash; v0.64</summary>
        <ul class="colophon-list">
          <li><strong>v0.64</strong> &mdash; an agent reading a claim through the tools gets the whole
            thing, not a preview: the drill-down views (provenance, the source-quote) now return a
            claim&rsquo;s full value instead of a four-hundred-character card, and a record&rsquo;s entry
            can be asked for full values too &mdash; so a long contribution argument reads complete over
            the reference desk and the machine interface alike, not just on the review screen.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.63</strong> &mdash; the focus view&rsquo;s verdict bar learns to speak: each
            verdict is a named lane with its own controls fused together and its consequence previewed
            under an &ldquo;if you do this&rdquo; rubric, the recommended lane visibly recommended &mdash;
            and the Review source pane renders the source as formatted text (the same escape-first
            markdown the Chat answer uses), with the claim&rsquo;s words highlighted where they appear.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.62</strong> &mdash; the two human queues get a room to decide in: Requests and
            Review keep their triage lists but gain a <em>focus view</em> &mdash; one proposal or one
            examined claim per screen, its evidence and its comparison (the map target&rsquo;s
            neighborhood; the claim beside its <em>full</em> source, fetched whole and highlighted where
            the claim&rsquo;s words appear) side by side, the verdict lanes always in reach with each
            lane&rsquo;s consequence stated in plain terms, arrow keys to move through the queue, and a
            verdict that advances to the next decision &mdash; and Review finally addresses every record
            by its human name rather than its identifier.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.61.1</strong> &mdash; the drain gauge learns to credit a claim re-read by an
            ordinary edit, not only by a deliberate revalidation, so a chapter already freshened when its
            source was last touched is not counted as still-owing &mdash; each visit now records whether
            it re-derived or merely inherited, and the gauge reads that.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.61</strong> &mdash; the fix reaches backward: v0.59 corrects a claim the next
            time its source is edited, but a claim already wrong on a chapter that is never touched again
            would stay wrong &mdash; so a deliberate <em>revalidation</em> pass re-reads a
            collection&rsquo;s claims against their current text and supersedes the ones that no longer
            hold, draining the sediment already in the store, with a gauge that reports how much
            remains.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.60</strong> &mdash; a claim stops calling itself <em>verified</em> when it only
            means the model was confident: a machine-asserted claim is now minted &ldquo;asserted,&rdquo;
            reserving &ldquo;verified&rdquo; for a claim a human stands behind, and every claim carries
            its honest <em>warrant</em> &mdash; whether an audit supported or contradicted it, whether a
            person or the model is behind it &mdash; so trust is read from the evidence rather than
            assumed from a reassuring label.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.59</strong> &mdash; a record whose source has changed stops inheriting its old
            reading: when an edit to the source triggers a re-tend, the reader is told the text has moved
            and re-derives each claim from the current words rather than confirming the prior one &mdash;
            the fix for a claim that went on saying &ldquo;improbable&rdquo; after its chapter had been
            rewritten to &ldquo;contingent,&rdquo; fresh but wrong &mdash; and the change signal can now
            be scoped per facet, so an edit that touches one dimension need not re-read them all.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.58</strong> &mdash; the first-impression diagnostic:
            <span class="mono">rake enliterator:first_impression</span> measures, in one number, how much
            a record&rsquo;s enliteration adds to a machine reader&rsquo;s first impression over the bare
            surrogate a catalog gives it &mdash; sampling records, generating a grounded question set,
            answering it under four reading conditions (abstract, catalog, abstract-plus-enliteration,
            raw source), blind-grading, and reporting the coverage and reliability lift with a canary
            that catches a leaked answer &mdash; the finding-aid thesis made falsifiable, that a stronger
            model cannot reason its way to knowledge that isn&rsquo;t in front of it.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.57.2</strong> &mdash; the heartbeat&rsquo;s budget box stops lying by omission:
            a value typed above the configured ceiling was silently clamped down to it, so raising it did
            nothing &mdash; now the form names the ceiling, warns live as you cross it, and the post-beat
            notice says when a request was clamped.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.57.1</strong> &mdash; a transient provider-serialization quirk (a double-encoded
            tool-call array, ~7% of long indexing reads) stops making permanent facet holes: named
            honestly, auto-retried with every attempt&rsquo;s spend on the ledger.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.57</strong> &mdash; the charter&rsquo;s telling half: the collection
            <em>introduces itself</em> &mdash; its proper noun, purpose, and audience, the extrinsic
            identity no synthesis can derive, told by its keepers as human-attributed claims on a one-row
            collection record, led with by every reader surface (the overview an agent reads first, the
            finding aid&rsquo;s header, the reference desk&rsquo;s own voice), with each still-untold
            field an open, named gap &mdash; the answer to the first external reader&rsquo;s complaint
            that the collection could report its skeleton but not say what it was.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.56</strong> &mdash; the topology declaration: a host tells the engine its
            collection&rsquo;s <em>shape</em> &mdash; the grouping that composes records into wholes
            (chapters into a book) &mdash; and the engine derives one context per whole (membership: the
            whole&rsquo;s full holdings, drafts included) and, when the collection declares itself an
            archive of sovereign works, reads each record against <em>its own whole</em> at tending time
            instead of the undifferentiated shelf &mdash; scoped by the host grouping itself, so a
            book&rsquo;s chapter meets its own book&rsquo;s chapters, never another&rsquo;s.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.55</strong> &mdash; the synthesized tendable: the engine&rsquo;s first
            <em>whole above records</em> &mdash; a host type that is a composite whole (a book made of
            its chapters) is tended by deliberate invocation and held out of the pacemaker and the corpus
            census, yet stays a real, drillable record whose book-level understanding still shows on
            every claim surface &mdash; &ldquo;the collection can think about itself,&rdquo; one level
            up.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.54.1</strong> &mdash; the review desk, finished: the evidence behind each
            proposal now names its source record by human title (not a uuid) and orders it in book
            position, and the per-record page shows full claim values instead of truncating them &mdash;
            the reviewer can finally read what they are judging.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.54</strong> &mdash; the review desk gives the reviewer what they need to decide:
            the full evidence behind each proposal &mdash; every record&rsquo;s reasoning and example, no
            truncation &mdash; is one click away, and a suggested mapping now shows what the target term
            means and what already folds onto it, so the fit can be judged before accepting.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.53</strong> &mdash; the review desk, reviewer-flow-first: each request card now
            leads with the considerer&rsquo;s recommended action &mdash; a suggested mapping puts a green
            Map first with the target already filled in, a suggested approval a green Approve &mdash; so
            a whole queue can be worked one click at a time, and the map target is a type-ahead over the
            vocabulary instead of a long dropdown.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.52</strong> &mdash; curator corrections: the standing vocabulary can now be
            re-adjudicated from the <%= link_to "authority file", vocabulary_path %> itself &mdash;
            re-route a variant onto a different preferred term, promote a variant to a preferred term,
            demote a preferred term, or merge a whole term and its variants into another &mdash; the work
            that previously required hand-editing the database.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.51</strong> &mdash; the <%= link_to "authority file", vocabulary_path %>: the
            standing controlled vocabulary drawn as a thesaurus &mdash; each preferred term with the
            variant terms folded onto it, ranked by how many it has gathered, so a curator can see the
            shape of the whole: a term absorbing variants from many facets, or a long tail of terms seen
            on a single record. The resolved companion to the Requests queue.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.50</strong> &mdash; the authority-control desk stops misleading the curator: the
            Map dropdown pre-fills the considerer&rsquo;s suggested target only when the considerer was
            confident enough to have applied it itself, a verdict that changes nothing now says so
            instead of reporting success, and a recommended target that is itself still pending explains
            that it must be approved before it can be mapped onto.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.49</strong> &mdash; the vocabulary desk stops blocking: the considerer reads the
            request field in batches and runs in the background, so &ldquo;Consider all requests&rdquo;
            returns at once and a live monitor shows it converge &mdash; a large queue no longer overruns
            a single giant call that times out.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.47</strong> &mdash; the atlas became a research instrument: alongside the
            orientation Overview, an Ego lens to explore around one record or entity &mdash; a ranked
            neighbor list beside the graph, an inspector showing a node&rsquo;s claims, provenance, and
            known gaps, and typed-edge filters by relation, confidence, audit, and depth, all sharing one
            selection.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.46.1</strong> &mdash; lacunae: the collection now records what it looked for and
            could not find &mdash; and why. When a required fact (an author, a date) comes back empty,
            the engine no longer files a blank claim that reads as a broken record &mdash; it opens a
            named gap, a known-unknown, diagnosed as to cause (the byline our extraction lost, a fact the
            document never carried, a name beyond recovery), and closes it the day a later reading
            supplies the value. A collection that can say not only what it knows but what it knows it is
            missing, and why.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.45</strong> &mdash; name authority control: the same person, written a dozen
            ways across the records (&ldquo;Jordan Avery,&rdquo; &ldquo;Jordan L. Avery,&rdquo;
            &ldquo;Jordan L. Avery (contractor)&rdquo;), now resolves to one authorized name, so a count
            is the person and not a spelling &mdash; the value-side twin of the vocabulary that tends
            itself, conservative by design (it folds the certain and holds the ambiguous, never merging
            two people).</li>
          <li><strong>v0.44</strong> &mdash; the desk can deliver: when the Reference Desk consults a
            record, it now names that record to the host as plain identity, so a federated front-end can
            turn &ldquo;what do you have on this?&rdquo; into a link to the catalog and, on request, the
            document itself &mdash; the desk stays read-only, it only surfaces.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.41.2</strong> &mdash; read-time warrant accrual: a reader now works with the
            vocabulary in hand, seeing the candidate terms other readers proposed and affirming one that
            fits rather than coining a synonym, so the controlled vocabulary converges where the reading
            happens.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.41.1</strong> &mdash; graceful bedrock unavailability: with all enliteration on
            bedrock and no fallback, an expired SSO session or a gateway timeout is a pause, not a
            failure &mdash; the heartbeat defers the work and resumes on the next beat.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.41</strong> &mdash; reset to seed: a desk returns to its registered persona,
            auditably &mdash; the persona editor, completed.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.40</strong> &mdash; the method, shipped: <em>enliterating-a-collection</em>, the
            practice of taking a collection from dormant to literate, encoded as a first-draft skill in
            the gem to be tended through every use.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.39</strong> &mdash; chat retention: every conversation kept as a first-class
            artifact and replayed from its own event stream, so the desk's conversations become
            tendable.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.38</strong> &mdash; the desk made testable: a per-desk step budget and a
            no-browser way to evaluate a desk as data.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.37</strong> &mdash; persona editing: each desk's voice is curator-editable,
            versioned, and reversible &mdash; code seeds, the store governs.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.36</strong> &mdash; the reference register: an engine-owned institutional voice
            beneath every desk's persona.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.35</strong> &mdash; the desk helps you ask: the agent reasons its own next
            questions, clickable.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.34</strong> &mdash; follow the stream: the answer auto-scrolls and the sources
            tuck away.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.33</strong> &mdash; the agentic answer streams token-by-token.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.32</strong> &mdash; a consulted record becomes a door back into the
            conversation.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.31</strong> &mdash; the reading room quiets: the chrome recedes as you talk and
            the desk shows it's thinking.</li>
          <li><strong>v0.30</strong> &mdash; actionable errors at the desk: when a turn fails, the
